# what: the template for .env. Copy it, fill it in, never commit the copy.
# why:  .env is in .gitignore; this file is not. A teammate cloning the repo
#       gets the SHAPE of the configuration without ever getting a secret, and
#       nobody has to guess which variables exist.
# how:  `cp .env.example .env`, then fill in ONE of the three provider blocks.
#       docker-compose.yml passes this file to the agent container, so what is
#       below is what the deployed agent actually reads.

# =============================================================================
# 1. WHICH MODEL ADAPTER run.py BUILDS
# =============================================================================
# mock   -> MockLLM: a fixed script. No network, no cost. Use this FIRST on a
#           new machine to prove Docker, the exporter, Prometheus, the detector,
#           the allowlist, the audit trail and the checkpoint file all work
#           before adding a model to the picture.
# openai -> OpenAIAdapter: a real HTTP call to whatever LLM_BASE_URL names.
#           Despite the name it is not OpenAI-specific; it speaks the
#           /chat/completions dialect, which is what Qwen servers speak too.
#
# Neither of these is how you demonstrate the HUMAN GATE. For that use the
# --rehearse flag, which scripts a restart request and is also free:
#     docker compose run --rm agent python -m agent.run --rehearse
# The flag exists because since Stage 4 a real model usually DECLINES to
# restart -- correct behaviour, and a terrible demo.
LLM_PROVIDER=mock

# =============================================================================
# 2a. A LOCAL QWEN SERVER  <-- the VM deployment uses this one
# =============================================================================
# Ollama, vLLM, llama.cpp's server and LM Studio all expose an OpenAI-compatible
# /v1/chat/completions, which is the only endpoint this project uses. So a local
# Qwen needs NO new adapter -- it needs a handful of environment variables.
#
# The base URL must end in /v1 and must be reachable FROM INSIDE the agent
# container. Pick the form that matches where the model runs:
#   same VM, on the host      ->  http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1
#   another machine on the LAN->  http://10.0.0.NN:11434/v1
#   a container in this stack ->  http://<service-name>:11434/v1
# "localhost" inside a container means the container itself, so it is almost
# always the wrong answer here.
#
# LLM_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1
# LLM_MODEL=qwen2.5:7b-instruct
#
# A local server usually wants no credential at all. The adapter refuses to
# start without one by default -- a missing key should stop a run at start-up,
# not halfway through an investigation -- so say so explicitly:
# LLM_REQUIRE_AUTH=false
#
# Ask the SERVER for JSON. Small local models are much likelier to wrap their
# answer in prose or, on Qwen3, in a <think> block; either makes Pydantic reject
# the reply and costs one of only five steps. This is provider CONFIGURATION,
# not us editing the model's answer -- the adapter still hands back whatever
# arrives, untouched. Ollama and vLLM both accept json_object. If yours answers
# 400, leave it unset and disable thinking at the model end instead.
# LLM_RESPONSE_FORMAT=json_object
#
# A 7B model on CPU can take well over a minute for one answer. The default is
# 60 seconds, generous for a hosted API and tight for a local one.
# LLM_TIMEOUT=180
#
# Most local servers accept temperature 0 and benefit from it here.
# LLM_TEMPERATURE=0

# =============================================================================
# 2b. The course gateway  (ask Luis for the two CF values)
# =============================================================================
# Measured 19 Sep: this returned 403 from Cloudflare Access with the team's
# credentials. Left here because it is the documented course route.
# LLM_BASE_URL=https://ia.pezcol.dev/v1
# LLM_MODEL=llm-luis
# CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID=
# CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET=

# =============================================================================
# 2c. The team's own OpenAI key  (this one spends the USD 40)
# =============================================================================
# LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# LLM_MODEL=gpt-5.6-luna
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# =============================================================================
# 3. WHERE THE AGENT KEEPS ITS INFRASTRUCTURE AND ITS STATE
# =============================================================================
# All four are already set in docker-compose.yml for the containerised agent.
# They appear here so that running `python -m agent.run` on a host works too:
# the defaults below are a laptop's, not a container's.
#
# The last one is the Stage 5 one, and it is the one that bites. The human gate
# works by SAVING the run and exiting; the approval is a second process. If the
# checkpoint file is not somewhere that outlives the container, the pause is
# gone before anyone can answer it -- and the symptom is not an error, it is
# "Nothing is waiting for an answer".
#
# PROMETHEUS_URL=http://prometheus:9090
# LAB_AGENT_DB=/data/lab_agent.db
# LAB_AGENT_AUDIT=/data/audit.jsonl
# LAB_AGENT_CHECKPOINTS=/data/lab_checkpoints.db
